# config: mock-noeval.json
#
# The analysis role inherited from "kibitz" by an engine that cannot analyse.
# Not an invented case: GNU Go has neither kata-analyze nor lz-analyze and
# carries "kibitz" in the shipped config, so this is what a stock install does.
#
# kibitz needs only genmove, which every engine has, so inheriting the analysis
# role assumes a capability it never had to possess. The engine is asked at load,
# and the answer decides whether the feature is offered at all.
#
#   ./tests/run_scenarios.sh tests/scenarios/evaluation_unavailable.scn

new_game 9
wait_idle

# Not offered. Nobody designated this engine for analysis, and it was asked at
# load rather than by spawning a clone of it to be told the same thing — so the
# toggle is greyed rather than inviting the user to switch on a feature that
# cannot work.
expect can_evaluation false
expect eval_state unavailable

# Nothing is displayed. -1 rather than 50 is the point: an empty bar at the
# midpoint cannot be told from a genuine even game.
expect eval_winrate -1
expect eval_has_score false
expect eval_moves 0

# And quietly: nothing was asked for, so nothing failed. The log says what is
# unavailable and why, at info. An engine *designated* with "analysis": 1 that
# cannot analyse does warn — see evaluation_engine_cannot_analyse.scn.
expect log_badge none

# The game itself is untouched — the analysis engine never shared a pipe with
# the playing one, so its failure cannot reach the board.
#
# `wait_until`, not `wait_idle` then `expect`: a click hands the move to a
# LocalHumanPlayer blocked in genmove(), and until the game loop wakes and picks
# it up nothing is thinking, queued or deferred — so isIdle() is momentarily true
# with the move not yet in the record. And White is the mock here, so the count
# does not stop at 1 either.
click 2 2
click 2 2
wait_until move_count >= 1
expect phase playing
